\mt1 EDITOR’S INTRODUCTION
\ip The Old Testament is eBible.org’s transcription of Sir Lancelot Charles Lee Brenton’s English translation of the Septuagint. It follows the text of 1870, with the corrections that eBible has made to it, and keeps Brenton’s preface, introduction, introduction to the Apocrypha, abbreviations, notes, and appendices.
\ip The New Testament is the twenty-seven books of eBible.org’s KJV Cambridge Paragraph Bible, together with the Epistle Dedicatory and The Translators to the Reader. The historical Cambridge title page has been left out, and Scrivener’s critical introduction and scholarly appendices, which are not part of the digital edition, do not appear here. Both sources describe their texts as public domain; eBible’s KJV notice also records the Crown’s continuing printing rights in the United Kingdom.
\ip The volume is in two parts, and each opens with the front matter of its own translation. The Old Testament begins with Brenton’s preface of 1844, his introduction of 1870, his introduction to the Apocrypha, and his list of the abbreviations and signs used in the notes. The New Testament begins with the Epistle Dedicatory and The Translators to the Reader. Closing notes stay with the books they belong to. After Revelation come three appendices: Brenton’s table of chapters and verses in Jeremias, his notes and supplied passages, and eBible’s corrections to the text.
\ip The books are arranged in the Old Testament order of the Church of Greece, with the Prayer of Manasses after 2 Chronicles and 4 Maccabees after 3 Maccabees. Brenton’s source labels its combined book “Ezra and Nehemiah”; here it is divided after chapter 10 into 2 Esdras and Nehemias 1–13, and the separate source file labelled “Nehemiah,” which duplicates that material, is omitted. The additions to Esther, the Song of the Three Children in Daniel 3, and Psalm 151 are kept. Susanna comes before Daniel 1 and Bel and the Dragon after Daniel 12, both as parts of Daniel; the running heads number them Daniel 0 and Daniel 13. Susanna, the Song of the Three Children, and Bel and the Dragon each have a heading added by this edition. Malachias 3:19–24 is numbered 4:1–6, and the cross-reference note at 3:23 is labelled 4:5 to match.
\ip The wording and notes of the sources have not been changed. Added words are set in regular type. Apart from the changes to Ezra–Nehemias, Daniel, and Malachias just described, the chapter and verse numbers are those of the sources. The book order, the title pages, the section dividers, and the table of contents are this edition’s own.
\ip So are several of the book names. Brenton’s Joshua is here Jesus, the Son of Navi; his Jezekiel is Ezekiel and his Michæas is Michaias; his Kings I–IV are 1–4 Kingdoms; his Song of Solomon is the Song of Songs; and his Lamentations is the Lamentations of Jeremias. “(Greek)” is dropped from Esther and Daniel. Each book is then named in two ways. The table of contents gives its full title in the manner of the Cambridge KJV, whose Genesis is “The First Book of Moses, Called Genesis”; Brenton’s books are titled the same way, and so are the few the Cambridge Bible lacks, so the contents list “The Book of Jesus, the Son of Navi,” “The Third Book of Kingdoms, Otherwise Called, The First Book of the Kings,” and “The Wisdom of Jesus, the Son of Sirach, or, Ecclesiasticus.” The running heads use a short name, with the number first where there is one: Genesis, 3 Kingdoms, 1 Maccabees, Sirach. The heading over each book is its full title, broken into lines as the Cambridge KJV breaks it. In the New Testament, “S.” is written out as “Saint,” Paul the Apostle is Saint Paul, and the seven Catholic Epistles, including the second and third of John, take their group name from the Greek tradition. Closing full stops have been removed from titles and headings, and a few headings in the front matter and appendices have been reworded. The opinions expressed in the introductions and appendices are those of their original authors.
\ip The volume was typeset with PTXprint 3.0.43 in its BSB A5 layout. The text is set in Utopia, with GFS Porson for Greek and Ezra SIL for Hebrew. This is an English Bible; Greek and Hebrew appear only in the introductions, notes, and appendices.
